WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:03.350
- Learning a language is
kind of like a video game.

00:00:03.350 --> 00:00:06.500
I mean kind of, not exactly the same,

00:00:06.500 --> 00:00:07.875
but let me explain.

00:00:07.875 --> 00:00:11.125
(tranquil piano music)

00:00:13.990 --> 00:00:15.833
I'm not a big gamer.

00:00:16.680 --> 00:00:19.040
I've played my fair share
of Super Smash Brothers,

00:00:19.040 --> 00:00:20.750
but that was like when I was a teenager.

00:00:20.750 --> 00:00:22.920
But lately, I'm getting back into it.

00:00:22.920 --> 00:00:24.020
We got a Nintendo Switch.

00:00:24.020 --> 00:00:25.580
I've been playing it with my boys.

00:00:25.580 --> 00:00:28.130
Zelda, this cool game called Cardo

00:00:28.130 --> 00:00:29.870
has to do with maps, good times.

00:00:29.870 --> 00:00:31.870
Another thing I've been
doing during quarantine

00:00:31.870 --> 00:00:34.410
is not traveling.

00:00:34.410 --> 00:00:36.810
Last fall when I started realizing

00:00:36.810 --> 00:00:38.020
that COVID wasn't going away,

00:00:38.020 --> 00:00:40.330
I would not be traveling anytime soon.

00:00:40.330 --> 00:00:42.260
I decided to get my travel buzz

00:00:42.260 --> 00:00:45.360
by learning a new language, Italian.

00:00:45.360 --> 00:00:49.120
The language that is spoken
in my favorite region on Earth

00:00:49.120 --> 00:00:49.953
right up here.

00:00:52.130 --> 00:00:55.830
Four months ago, I didn't
speak a word of Italian

00:00:55.830 --> 00:00:57.780
and today, (speaking foreign language).

00:01:13.587 --> 00:01:15.200
So I've been driving back from work

00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:19.490
and speaking to myself in Italian.

00:01:19.490 --> 00:01:22.010
I am feeling my head.

00:01:22.010 --> 00:01:24.727
There's like a physical
buzzing that happens.

00:01:24.727 --> 00:01:29.120
(speaking foreign language)

00:01:29.120 --> 00:01:30.540
For the past three months,

00:01:30.540 --> 00:01:32.670
I've been studying Italian in the morning

00:01:32.670 --> 00:01:36.140
and playing video games
with my kids at night.

00:01:36.140 --> 00:01:38.960
I recently started to realize
how some of the major lessons

00:01:38.960 --> 00:01:42.840
from video games can be applied
to learning a new language

00:01:42.840 --> 00:01:45.600
and to do so in a much more efficient way

00:01:45.600 --> 00:01:47.870
than was taught to us in school.

00:01:47.870 --> 00:01:50.590
By the end of this video, I
want to explain this concept

00:01:50.590 --> 00:01:53.500
of video games and language learning

00:01:53.500 --> 00:01:55.350
and I want to give you my best takeaways

00:01:55.350 --> 00:01:57.640
on teaching yourself a new language.

00:01:57.640 --> 00:01:59.020
So back to video games.

00:01:59.020 --> 00:02:02.673
My favorite video games
are ones with maps.

00:02:04.390 --> 00:02:06.280
You'd be surprised, he's a big map guy.

00:02:06.280 --> 00:02:09.150
He loves maps, I know,
I'm like a walking cliche.

00:02:09.150 --> 00:02:10.363
I just like maps.

00:02:11.650 --> 00:02:15.270
So if learning a language is
sort of like a video game,

00:02:15.270 --> 00:02:17.520
here's the map of the video game.

00:02:17.520 --> 00:02:20.310
This is the learning language journey.

00:02:20.310 --> 00:02:23.820
You start here knowing nothing
about your new language

00:02:23.820 --> 00:02:26.800
and your goal is to beat the final boss,

00:02:26.800 --> 00:02:30.200
this elusive idea of fluency.

00:02:30.200 --> 00:02:32.380
In other words, when you start your Link

00:02:32.380 --> 00:02:34.450
and you just woke up from 100 year nap

00:02:34.450 --> 00:02:37.190
and you have three hearts
and no weapons and no stamina

00:02:37.190 --> 00:02:39.860
and no skills and no powers

00:02:39.860 --> 00:02:42.720
and you don't speak the language,

00:02:42.720 --> 00:02:45.940
where you want to be is like this.

00:02:45.940 --> 00:02:49.320
Having lots of skills and tools
to navigate a conversation

00:02:49.320 --> 00:02:53.240
with precision and
skill and beat the game.

00:02:53.240 --> 00:02:56.060
In other words, to become fluent.

00:02:56.060 --> 00:02:57.440
Wait, wait, wait, wait, wait.

00:02:57.440 --> 00:03:00.980
This is a moment, this is a
thing I need to distinguish,

00:03:00.980 --> 00:03:02.900
a distinction we need to make.

00:03:02.900 --> 00:03:04.640
What is fluency?

00:03:04.640 --> 00:03:05.700
What does that even mean?

00:03:05.700 --> 00:03:08.180
I'll turn the question
on you gamers out there.

00:03:08.180 --> 00:03:11.070
What does it mean to beat a video game?

00:03:11.070 --> 00:03:13.040
One person will tell you
that beating a video game

00:03:13.040 --> 00:03:15.400
means just beating the main storyline,

00:03:15.400 --> 00:03:18.870
beating the main boss,
Ganon, Bowser, whatever.

00:03:18.870 --> 00:03:21.080
I can already feel some
of you shaking your heads

00:03:21.080 --> 00:03:24.070
because there are lots of you
out there that say no, no, no,

00:03:24.070 --> 00:03:26.900
beating a video game
means beating everything.

00:03:26.900 --> 00:03:30.060
All of the challenges,
all of the little things

00:03:30.060 --> 00:03:32.930
like beating the shrines and
getting all the Korok seeds

00:03:32.930 --> 00:03:34.820
and all the other things
that have nothing to do

00:03:34.820 --> 00:03:36.390
with the main storyline.

00:03:36.390 --> 00:03:39.530
You have to beat them all or
you haven't beaten the game.

00:03:39.530 --> 00:03:42.040
So here's where the connection
between language learning

00:03:42.040 --> 00:03:45.300
and the video game concept comes together.

00:03:45.300 --> 00:03:47.410
When you're learning a
language in language school

00:03:47.410 --> 00:03:51.033
or most formal programs,
the map looks like this.

00:03:57.980 --> 00:03:59.160
You start here knowing nothing

00:03:59.160 --> 00:04:02.460
and the journey to beat
the game to become fluent

00:04:02.460 --> 00:04:07.050
is a long windy path with lots of gates

00:04:07.050 --> 00:04:09.170
and trials and gatekeepers.

00:04:09.170 --> 00:04:11.820
There are soldiers who are standing guard,

00:04:11.820 --> 00:04:13.440
making sure that you beat them

00:04:13.440 --> 00:04:15.370
before you go on to the next step.

00:04:15.370 --> 00:04:17.550
You can't progress along this path

00:04:17.550 --> 00:04:20.090
until you've memorized how to conjugate

00:04:20.090 --> 00:04:24.070
the present imperative tense
including the irregular forms.

00:04:24.070 --> 00:04:26.260
So you study them, you study, study, study

00:04:26.260 --> 00:04:28.320
just to make it pass
this first gatekeeper.

00:04:28.320 --> 00:04:30.880
You don't even really know
what present imperative means

00:04:30.880 --> 00:04:33.230
but you look at the book, you memorize it

00:04:33.230 --> 00:04:34.820
and you do what you need to do to show up

00:04:34.820 --> 00:04:36.890
and pass the test so that you can move on

00:04:36.890 --> 00:04:38.860
to the next thing that
they're teaching you

00:04:38.860 --> 00:04:40.680
in this school formal environment.

00:04:40.680 --> 00:04:43.480
So then you move on and you
get to the next gatekeeper

00:04:43.480 --> 00:04:45.490
in the trial, which is you can't move on

00:04:45.490 --> 00:04:48.830
until you've memorized the
subjunctive conjugations.

00:04:48.830 --> 00:04:50.180
And you're like what
does subjunctive mean?

00:04:50.180 --> 00:04:51.510
And it's just like I don't know,

00:04:51.510 --> 00:04:52.880
but you need to go memorize it.

00:04:52.880 --> 00:04:54.450
Oh, and memorize this random list

00:04:54.450 --> 00:04:56.720
of 20 words that we think are important.

00:04:56.720 --> 00:04:58.750
It's the only way to fluency.

00:04:58.750 --> 00:05:02.113
If you want to be fluent, you
have to get through this gate.

00:05:03.670 --> 00:05:05.200
I'll memorize the subjunctive.

00:05:05.200 --> 00:05:07.890
You keep going on your journey,
passing the gatekeepers,

00:05:07.890 --> 00:05:10.640
memorizing the concepts
that are in the book

00:05:10.640 --> 00:05:12.010
that the teacher is telling you

00:05:12.010 --> 00:05:13.730
and the idea here is that if you beat

00:05:13.730 --> 00:05:16.440
all of these gatekeepers and
you memorize all these rules,

00:05:16.440 --> 00:05:17.920
you make it to the final level

00:05:17.920 --> 00:05:22.330
and you beat the final boss
and then you are fluent.

00:05:22.330 --> 00:05:25.370
The problem is this
isn't a fun video game.

00:05:25.370 --> 00:05:27.410
It's actually a grueling process

00:05:27.410 --> 00:05:30.130
of memorizing abstract ideas.

00:05:30.130 --> 00:05:32.900
So people don't actually beat
the video game very often.

00:05:32.900 --> 00:05:37.200
They usually give up around
here and they say F it

00:05:37.200 --> 00:05:38.660
and they throw in the towel

00:05:38.660 --> 00:05:42.980
and they feel like language
learning is just not for them.

00:05:42.980 --> 00:05:45.150
Meanwhile, some of us beat the game.

00:05:45.150 --> 00:05:47.220
Like I actually beat the game in college.

00:05:47.220 --> 00:05:50.090
I got a minor in French, like a minor.

00:05:50.090 --> 00:05:53.180
Like I studied French
through all of the levels

00:05:53.180 --> 00:05:54.340
in college to get a minor.

00:05:54.340 --> 00:05:57.410
I beat this game and I
finally beat the final boss

00:05:57.410 --> 00:05:59.140
and got here to the end of the map

00:05:59.140 --> 00:06:02.320
and I had all the conjugations,
the direct object pronouns,

00:06:02.320 --> 00:06:05.280
the past conditional and
auxiliary verbs and all of it.

00:06:05.280 --> 00:06:08.760
And then I go to Paris and
I go to order a baguette

00:06:08.760 --> 00:06:10.940
and I realized that I literally don't know

00:06:10.940 --> 00:06:13.820
how to order a baguette in Paris

00:06:13.820 --> 00:06:15.700
even though I have a minor in French.

00:06:15.700 --> 00:06:19.650
There is nothing more
disappointing than that, it's sad.

00:06:19.650 --> 00:06:22.260
Luckily, there's a happy
ending to this video,

00:06:22.260 --> 00:06:24.080
this story that I'm telling you,

00:06:24.080 --> 00:06:27.070
but right now, it's just sad
to think about getting a minor

00:06:27.070 --> 00:06:31.710
in French and then not actually
being able to speak it.

00:06:31.710 --> 00:06:33.200
I need a little bit of a change of pace,

00:06:33.200 --> 00:06:35.163
I'm gonna crack open a bottle of wine.

00:06:36.300 --> 00:06:37.980
Which begins the next segment

00:06:37.980 --> 00:06:39.790
which is thanking today's sponsor

00:06:39.790 --> 00:06:43.770
who sent me a giant box
full of literal wine.

00:06:43.770 --> 00:06:45.793
Look at this.

00:06:47.000 --> 00:06:48.410
Whoops, check this out.

00:06:48.410 --> 00:06:50.590
This box arrived at my door two days ago.

00:06:50.590 --> 00:06:54.033
Inside of the box, there
are some really cool things.

00:06:55.190 --> 00:06:57.930
So Bright Cellars who
sponsored today's video

00:06:57.930 --> 00:07:00.010
sent me this box.

00:07:00.010 --> 00:07:03.100
They had me take a quiz
before on things that I like,

00:07:03.100 --> 00:07:07.153
the type of food and the
types of personality traits

00:07:07.153 --> 00:07:09.520
I have like all this whole quiz.

00:07:09.520 --> 00:07:12.590
And then they used the
information from that quiz

00:07:12.590 --> 00:07:15.220
to send me this box of wine.

00:07:15.220 --> 00:07:18.430
What I like about this is
because when I go to buy wine

00:07:18.430 --> 00:07:20.840
at a store, I have no guidance,

00:07:20.840 --> 00:07:21.947
like there's no guidance for me.

00:07:21.947 --> 00:07:25.400
I'm not super well-versed in
all of the different variables

00:07:25.400 --> 00:07:26.250
that make good wine.

00:07:26.250 --> 00:07:30.050
I'd like to be, but
it's big and complicated

00:07:30.050 --> 00:07:32.630
and for someone to automate
that and to present it

00:07:32.630 --> 00:07:34.350
in a way that's fun

00:07:34.350 --> 00:07:36.930
and like sort of empowering
with information,

00:07:36.930 --> 00:07:38.980
that's actually my favorite
part about the whole thing

00:07:38.980 --> 00:07:42.670
is they will send me these
cards for each bottle of wine

00:07:42.670 --> 00:07:45.220
that has a little infographic on it.

00:07:45.220 --> 00:07:47.550
I like infographics, that's like my thing.

00:07:47.550 --> 00:07:50.450
There's maps and there is origins

00:07:50.450 --> 00:07:53.650
and there's a story about
like where this came from.

00:07:53.650 --> 00:07:57.050
There's flavor notes if you're
into the flavor notes game.

00:07:57.050 --> 00:08:00.100
For me, ritualistic things
like cheese and wine

00:08:00.100 --> 00:08:03.030
and all of it is way more enjoyable

00:08:03.030 --> 00:08:06.030
if there's a story associated with it.

00:08:06.030 --> 00:08:08.520
Bright Cellars is giving 50% off,

00:08:08.520 --> 00:08:11.530
which is half off for
anyone who clicked the link

00:08:11.530 --> 00:08:13.210
in my description for
your first six bottles.

00:08:13.210 --> 00:08:15.250
So you could sign up for this sweet thing,

00:08:15.250 --> 00:08:18.630
get six bottles for 50% off
and it's a really good deal.

00:08:18.630 --> 00:08:20.620
And again, the convenience,
the information,

00:08:20.620 --> 00:08:22.710
the experience of this is really cool.

00:08:22.710 --> 00:08:24.540
I'm excited, I'm excited to explore this

00:08:24.540 --> 00:08:26.950
and have some knowledge and
story associated with it.

00:08:26.950 --> 00:08:29.090
So thank you Bright Cellars
for sponsoring this video.

00:08:29.090 --> 00:08:30.460
Let's get back to the story

00:08:30.460 --> 00:08:34.603
of learning Italian
through a video game map.

00:08:37.200 --> 00:08:38.400
I beat the game.

00:08:38.400 --> 00:08:42.570
I got here and realized
that this version of fluency

00:08:42.570 --> 00:08:46.580
was one where I had a deep
understanding of French grammar.

00:08:46.580 --> 00:08:48.800
I even had an understanding
of its history,

00:08:48.800 --> 00:08:50.550
I could read literature in French,

00:08:50.550 --> 00:08:51.720
I could write properly,

00:08:51.720 --> 00:08:53.090
I knew where all the accents went,

00:08:53.090 --> 00:08:55.510
I even had a decent pronunciation

00:08:55.510 --> 00:08:57.430
but I couldn't speak the damn language.

00:08:57.430 --> 00:09:00.240
When I traveled to France,
wasn't that the whole point?

00:09:00.240 --> 00:09:01.560
For all you Zelda people out here,

00:09:01.560 --> 00:09:04.090
let's just say I had
gathered all the Korok seeds

00:09:04.090 --> 00:09:05.570
and beat all the shrines

00:09:05.570 --> 00:09:08.830
but still hadn't beat
a single divine beast.

00:09:08.830 --> 00:09:10.330
The thing that actually mattered to me,

00:09:10.330 --> 00:09:12.010
the thing I wanted to do,

00:09:12.010 --> 00:09:13.900
what if I decided that this

00:09:13.900 --> 00:09:15.733
isn't where I wanted to go at all?

00:09:16.800 --> 00:09:18.860
This version of fluency wasn't important.

00:09:18.860 --> 00:09:20.670
This isn't the game I want to play.

00:09:20.670 --> 00:09:24.130
What if we decided to
take a different approach,

00:09:24.130 --> 00:09:27.060
a different path, a much simpler goal.

00:09:27.060 --> 00:09:28.880
And what if that goal were as simple

00:09:28.880 --> 00:09:32.700
as I want to be able to
travel to a foreign country

00:09:32.700 --> 00:09:34.630
and be able to speak to the locals

00:09:34.630 --> 00:09:36.540
and have them understand me

00:09:36.540 --> 00:09:38.668
and be understood when they speak to me.

00:09:38.668 --> 00:09:42.130
Is that revolutionary like
no mastery of grammar,

00:09:42.130 --> 00:09:43.790
no perfect pronunciation,

00:09:43.790 --> 00:09:47.160
no ability to read and
write with any elegance,

00:09:47.160 --> 00:09:51.470
no literature or cultural
history of the language,

00:09:51.470 --> 00:09:54.500
just the ability to use
words to communicate

00:09:54.500 --> 00:09:56.850
and then to understand the response.

00:09:56.850 --> 00:10:00.380
Nothing else, no (squeaky
toy sound) Korok seeds.

00:10:00.380 --> 00:10:02.160
I can already hear the comments,

00:10:02.160 --> 00:10:04.110
but you're learning a language.

00:10:04.110 --> 00:10:06.710
You have to know the grammar
or you won't be fluent.

00:10:06.710 --> 00:10:07.620
Or in other words,

00:10:07.620 --> 00:10:08.990
if you don't gather all the Korok seeds,

00:10:08.990 --> 00:10:10.270
you didn't beat the game.

00:10:10.270 --> 00:10:14.170
Listen, if my definition
isn't fluency for you,

00:10:14.170 --> 00:10:16.360
I don't want your
fluency, I don't need it.

00:10:16.360 --> 00:10:18.210
I just want to be able to go on a trip

00:10:18.210 --> 00:10:20.780
and speak to the locals
and ask for directions

00:10:20.780 --> 00:10:23.910
and have a conversation with
a taxi driver and order food.

00:10:23.910 --> 00:10:25.290
That is all I want.

00:10:25.290 --> 00:10:26.330
I'm being a little snarky here

00:10:26.330 --> 00:10:28.550
because the language learning
community on the internet

00:10:28.550 --> 00:10:31.260
has some very strong
opinions and expectations

00:10:31.260 --> 00:10:34.100
about what real language
learning looks like.

00:10:34.100 --> 00:10:35.820
You may see them in
the comments telling me

00:10:35.820 --> 00:10:38.070
that my Italian actually isn't valid

00:10:38.070 --> 00:10:40.730
because I don't understand
the past conditional tense.

00:10:40.730 --> 00:10:41.990
Not sure how to respond to that,

00:10:41.990 --> 00:10:46.100
but I reject your objection.

00:10:46.100 --> 00:10:48.610
So a few months ago, I
set out to test this out

00:10:48.610 --> 00:10:52.140
to see what this destination looks like.

00:10:52.140 --> 00:10:53.910
Not this, but this.

00:10:53.910 --> 00:10:55.550
I learned a lot and ended up

00:10:55.550 --> 00:10:57.770
making an entire course about it

00:10:57.770 --> 00:10:59.030
with my friend Nathaniel Drew,

00:10:59.030 --> 00:11:01.980
but I want to summarize
the major things I learned

00:11:01.980 --> 00:11:04.359
and share them with you.

00:11:04.359 --> 00:11:08.026
(speaking foreign language)

00:11:18.257 --> 00:11:21.890
Before I dive in and give you
exactly the things I learned

00:11:21.890 --> 00:11:24.400
in this process, let
me do a few disclaimers

00:11:24.400 --> 00:11:25.810
that might be helpful to some of you

00:11:25.810 --> 00:11:29.600
who actually want to take away
some value from this video.

00:11:29.600 --> 00:11:31.750
Number one is that this is my experience.

00:11:31.750 --> 00:11:32.600
It's my experience,

00:11:32.600 --> 00:11:35.720
it applies to me and my unique situation.

00:11:35.720 --> 00:11:37.830
It worked well and it may work for you

00:11:37.830 --> 00:11:39.360
and it may not work for you,

00:11:39.360 --> 00:11:42.190
but don't see this as like
a plug and chug formula,

00:11:42.190 --> 00:11:43.660
that is not what this is.

00:11:43.660 --> 00:11:45.560
This is my perspective and my experience.

00:11:45.560 --> 00:11:47.230
Number two, I want it to be clear

00:11:47.230 --> 00:11:49.840
that I spoke literally zero Italian

00:11:49.840 --> 00:11:51.930
in like October of last year.

00:11:51.930 --> 00:11:54.640
I am fluent in Spanish,
which is a romance language,

00:11:54.640 --> 00:11:58.170
which is like a cousin to
Italian and I did study French

00:11:58.170 --> 00:12:00.840
in college as I've mentioned 57 times.

00:12:00.840 --> 00:12:02.580
So I had some advantage

00:12:02.580 --> 00:12:04.910
but the reality is I knew zero Italian.

00:12:04.910 --> 00:12:08.313
I could say things like
ciao and like that was it.

00:12:08.313 --> 00:12:11.980
(speaking foreign language)

00:12:34.270 --> 00:12:36.680
That's what matters,
that's what I care about.

00:12:36.680 --> 00:12:40.053
I rejected this and I
think there's a better way.

00:12:42.610 --> 00:12:44.050
That doesn't mean it's easy,

00:12:44.050 --> 00:12:46.620
it means that it's just more direct.

00:12:46.620 --> 00:12:48.240
There are still trials,

00:12:48.240 --> 00:12:51.210
they're just the most
efficient trials you need

00:12:51.210 --> 00:12:52.110
and here they are.

00:12:56.230 --> 00:12:59.770
Number one, not all
words are created equal.

00:12:59.770 --> 00:13:03.530
The first thing I did was to
assess what are the most common

00:13:03.530 --> 00:13:06.320
and useful 1,000 words in a language

00:13:06.320 --> 00:13:08.770
and solely focus on those,

00:13:08.770 --> 00:13:11.210
completely blocking out all others.

00:13:11.210 --> 00:13:13.120
This isn't a new concept.

00:13:13.120 --> 00:13:16.730
Scholarly research shows
that this is very useful

00:13:16.730 --> 00:13:18.470
and history does too.

00:13:18.470 --> 00:13:19.970
I last year was reading a lot

00:13:19.970 --> 00:13:23.730
about American imperialism
for obvious reasons

00:13:23.730 --> 00:13:25.840
and I stumbled upon this wild story.

00:13:25.840 --> 00:13:27.890
After World War II, the British

00:13:27.890 --> 00:13:29.540
and Americans who had just won the war

00:13:29.540 --> 00:13:31.581
wanted to spread the English language

00:13:31.581 --> 00:13:32.570
throughout the whole world.

00:13:32.570 --> 00:13:34.820
So they adopted a stripped down version

00:13:34.820 --> 00:13:36.870
that they called basic.

00:13:36.870 --> 00:13:39.050
They were carefully selected words.

00:13:39.050 --> 00:13:41.090
There were the most frequently used

00:13:41.090 --> 00:13:43.780
and most useful 850 words in the language.

00:13:43.780 --> 00:13:46.600
By the way, English has
like over 150,000 words.

00:13:46.600 --> 00:13:49.210
And this language created
by a linguist of English

00:13:49.210 --> 00:13:50.750
was only 850 words

00:13:50.750 --> 00:13:54.000
and they use this to go promulgate
English around the world.

00:13:54.000 --> 00:13:55.040
They're like anyone in the world

00:13:55.040 --> 00:13:56.550
can learn English very quickly

00:13:56.550 --> 00:13:58.910
if they just have to
learn these 850 words.

00:13:58.910 --> 00:14:01.640
The inventor of the
language once said quote

00:14:01.640 --> 00:14:06.170
it takes 400 words of
basic to run a battleship.

00:14:06.170 --> 00:14:09.790
And with 850 words,
you can run the planet.

00:14:09.790 --> 00:14:13.000
He literally said that like
this is an extreme version

00:14:13.000 --> 00:14:15.900
of what I'm talking about here.

00:14:15.900 --> 00:14:17.310
Some words are more important than others.

00:14:17.310 --> 00:14:19.240
And if you just memorize those words,

00:14:19.240 --> 00:14:21.260
those words that you
actually are gonna use,

00:14:21.260 --> 00:14:22.880
then you can like supercharge

00:14:22.880 --> 00:14:24.730
your language learning process.

00:14:24.730 --> 00:14:27.030
Now this is where my friend Nathaniel Drew

00:14:27.030 --> 00:14:28.320
comes into the story.

00:14:28.320 --> 00:14:32.060
Nathaniel is a guy who
loves languages like I do

00:14:32.060 --> 00:14:35.560
and he also believes in
this more direct path.

00:14:35.560 --> 00:14:38.650
So he's challenged
himself to learn as much

00:14:38.650 --> 00:14:42.730
of a new language as quickly
as he possibly can on his own.

00:14:42.730 --> 00:14:46.760
By the way, much to the
chagrin of language learning

00:14:46.760 --> 00:14:49.580
internet communities who love to say

00:14:49.580 --> 00:14:51.850
that people are learning
languages the wrong way

00:14:51.850 --> 00:14:53.570
because it doesn't fit their model.

00:14:53.570 --> 00:14:55.780
In doing so, Nathaniel
realized the same thing

00:14:55.780 --> 00:14:57.690
that British and American
governments realized

00:14:57.690 --> 00:14:58.840
in like the 40's

00:14:58.840 --> 00:15:01.530
that if you just prioritize
the most vital words,

00:15:01.530 --> 00:15:03.250
the ones that you actually use,

00:15:03.250 --> 00:15:05.570
you can rapidly acquire a language

00:15:05.570 --> 00:15:08.100
if you block out all of
the words and only focus

00:15:08.100 --> 00:15:09.400
on the most important.

00:15:09.400 --> 00:15:10.770
Nathaniel had made a list

00:15:10.770 --> 00:15:13.110
of what he deemed to be some
of the most important words

00:15:13.110 --> 00:15:15.550
for his life and for just
everyday communication.

00:15:15.550 --> 00:15:17.130
He gave me that list and I added to it.

00:15:17.130 --> 00:15:18.930
I did a bunch of research on the data

00:15:18.930 --> 00:15:20.550
of most frequently used words

00:15:20.550 --> 00:15:24.330
and I put it together
into a list of 1,000 words

00:15:24.330 --> 00:15:26.600
that I think are the startup kit,

00:15:26.600 --> 00:15:28.760
the most important words
you need to memorize.

00:15:28.760 --> 00:15:31.460
The ingredients, the
building blocks of language.

00:15:31.460 --> 00:15:32.860
This is the first step.

00:15:32.860 --> 00:15:34.310
Before you do anything else,

00:15:34.310 --> 00:15:37.600
memorize the most important
words of a language.

00:15:37.600 --> 00:15:39.640
How do you memorize all these words?

00:15:39.640 --> 00:15:40.870
I'm not gonna go into it right now.

00:15:40.870 --> 00:15:44.420
I have this box that I
use that has all my words

00:15:44.420 --> 00:15:46.280
and in the course that we made,

00:15:46.280 --> 00:15:48.190
I go into exactly how this thing works.

00:15:48.190 --> 00:15:50.210
It's based on all the psychology
that forces these words

00:15:50.210 --> 00:15:51.150
into your long-term memory.

00:15:51.150 --> 00:15:54.300
The point is memorize the most vital

00:15:54.300 --> 00:15:56.550
frequently used words first,

00:15:56.550 --> 00:15:58.640
and your life will be a lot easier

00:15:58.640 --> 00:16:00.040
in learning this language.

00:16:00.040 --> 00:16:03.870
I saw this just recently,
like I went from knowing

00:16:03.870 --> 00:16:07.010
zero words in Italian
to within the first week

00:16:07.010 --> 00:16:09.320
knowing 200 words of Italian.

00:16:09.320 --> 00:16:10.460
You can't speak super well

00:16:10.460 --> 00:16:12.400
with just 200 words and no grammar,

00:16:12.400 --> 00:16:16.063
but you can certainly
start to express yourself.

00:16:16.978 --> 00:16:20.645
(speaking foreign language)

00:16:26.100 --> 00:16:29.770
I kept memorizing and
soon I had like 500 words

00:16:29.770 --> 00:16:32.480
and you won't believe what 500 words

00:16:32.480 --> 00:16:35.070
can do for your ability to communicate.

00:16:35.070 --> 00:16:38.373
This gets to the second trial
on our video game adventure,

00:16:39.740 --> 00:16:42.800
which is start talking early.

00:16:42.800 --> 00:16:44.600
Language isn't math.

00:16:44.600 --> 00:16:46.070
In math, there are laws.

00:16:46.070 --> 00:16:49.150
If you break those laws,
your equation won't work.

00:16:49.150 --> 00:16:50.760
You literally get the wrong answer.

00:16:50.760 --> 00:16:53.130
There are wrong answers
and right answers in math.

00:16:53.130 --> 00:16:57.380
If my four year old son
says two plus two equals 22,

00:16:57.380 --> 00:16:59.770
I will say no, son, that's wrong.

00:16:59.770 --> 00:17:01.970
Two plus two equals four.

00:17:01.970 --> 00:17:06.950
But if my son says
yesterday, I eated a apple.

00:17:06.950 --> 00:17:11.670
I wouldn't say no son,
yesterday you ate an apple.

00:17:11.670 --> 00:17:12.890
Get it right next time.

00:17:12.890 --> 00:17:14.590
No, I wouldn't say that.

00:17:14.590 --> 00:17:16.940
The kid said words, I understood them

00:17:16.940 --> 00:17:19.420
and it worked, that's
language, it isn't math.

00:17:19.420 --> 00:17:22.590
It's an expressive part
of our human experience

00:17:22.590 --> 00:17:26.250
that is very intuitive and
very flexible and messy

00:17:26.250 --> 00:17:29.280
like human culture and relationships.

00:17:29.280 --> 00:17:30.550
It is not math.

00:17:30.550 --> 00:17:33.093
When we approach language like this,

00:17:36.060 --> 00:17:38.490
we make language feel like math.

00:17:38.490 --> 00:17:40.770
Like if we don't get the
conjugations correct,

00:17:40.770 --> 00:17:43.570
it'll be like we're saying
two plus two equals 22

00:17:43.570 --> 00:17:45.737
and you'll be totally wrong
and no one will understand you.

00:17:45.737 --> 00:17:48.530
And all that does is make
you nervous and averse

00:17:48.530 --> 00:17:51.140
to actually speaking in real time.

00:17:51.140 --> 00:17:51.973
But watch this.

00:17:51.973 --> 00:17:54.630
I'm about to show you me speaking Italian

00:17:54.630 --> 00:17:57.370
a month and a half, six
weeks after studying it,

00:17:57.370 --> 00:18:01.100
going from zero words to a few 100 words

00:18:01.100 --> 00:18:02.830
and trying to speak it.

00:18:02.830 --> 00:18:05.930
Speaking with a native in Italy over Skype

00:18:05.930 --> 00:18:08.640
and spoiler alert for
those Italian speakers,

00:18:08.640 --> 00:18:10.854
my grammar is horrible.

00:18:10.854 --> 00:18:14.604
(speaking foreign language)

00:18:33.150 --> 00:18:36.490
But guess what, she understood me.

00:18:36.490 --> 00:18:40.490
I was communicating in a
different language with somebody.

00:18:40.490 --> 00:18:41.670
It's not pretty,

00:18:41.670 --> 00:18:45.080
it's not linguistically
correct, but it's communication.

00:18:45.080 --> 00:18:47.740
What I'm proposing is that
this is the alternative path,

00:18:47.740 --> 00:18:51.460
this is the goal, not getting
the mathematical equations

00:18:51.460 --> 00:18:53.410
of grammar and syntax correct.

00:18:53.410 --> 00:18:54.870
As you memorize loads of words,

00:18:54.870 --> 00:18:57.290
the next most important step
is to get yourself speaking

00:18:57.290 --> 00:18:59.010
as soon as possible.

00:18:59.010 --> 00:19:00.230
You'll have to do this eventually

00:19:00.230 --> 00:19:01.610
if you want to speak the language

00:19:01.610 --> 00:19:03.220
and it's awkward and painful.

00:19:03.220 --> 00:19:06.990
And so you should start
early right away on week one

00:19:06.990 --> 00:19:09.510
of learning the language
and you should get on

00:19:09.510 --> 00:19:10.810
one of these services

00:19:10.810 --> 00:19:14.190
and start communicating
with a native speaker.

00:19:14.190 --> 00:19:16.840
And now I'm about to say
something that actually pains me

00:19:16.840 --> 00:19:19.820
which is after two months of this,

00:19:19.820 --> 00:19:21.830
just memorizing tons of words

00:19:21.830 --> 00:19:24.800
and having weekly Skype
conversations with a native Italian,

00:19:24.800 --> 00:19:27.190
I was speaking better Italian

00:19:27.190 --> 00:19:30.200
more fluently than I was French.

00:19:30.200 --> 00:19:34.030
I eclipsed my French capabilities
from a verbal standpoint,

00:19:34.030 --> 00:19:35.660
not from a grammar standpoint,

00:19:35.660 --> 00:19:37.890
but from a verbal expressive standpoint

00:19:37.890 --> 00:19:42.260
after two months, compared
to four years of work.

00:19:42.260 --> 00:19:43.880
I'm not saying it was
fluent after two months,

00:19:43.880 --> 00:19:47.060
I'm just saying that my
experience after two months

00:19:47.060 --> 00:19:50.250
was stronger than my four
years of college French.

00:19:50.250 --> 00:19:52.560
That's like actually
sad for me in some ways

00:19:52.560 --> 00:19:55.410
because there's so much
time of me learning French

00:19:55.410 --> 00:19:58.140
and there's a better way.

00:19:58.140 --> 00:19:59.200
In the course that I mentioned,

00:19:59.200 --> 00:20:01.570
we go into a deep dive on
all the types of activities

00:20:01.570 --> 00:20:03.010
that I do in one of these sessions

00:20:03.010 --> 00:20:05.310
with a native speaker
that helps make it useful,

00:20:05.310 --> 00:20:07.380
but I'm not gonna go into that right now.

00:20:07.380 --> 00:20:09.810
Gate number three, you
got to make this fun.

00:20:09.810 --> 00:20:14.810
Another major thing missing
from this model is fun.

00:20:15.230 --> 00:20:17.430
Positive association is how it's called

00:20:17.430 --> 00:20:18.940
in behavioral psychology.

00:20:18.940 --> 00:20:22.950
The idea that your brain
wants to do something

00:20:22.950 --> 00:20:25.070
if last time they did
it, it was sort of fun.

00:20:25.070 --> 00:20:26.320
Language learning is hard,

00:20:26.320 --> 00:20:28.170
it requires many months and years.

00:20:28.170 --> 00:20:29.210
How do you keep it up?

00:20:29.210 --> 00:20:31.030
You make it interesting and fun.

00:20:31.030 --> 00:20:34.000
Grammar drills and
memorization are not fun

00:20:34.000 --> 00:20:38.660
so you have to find ways to
make it a positive experience.

00:20:38.660 --> 00:20:41.520
For me, that is reading
an Italian cookbook

00:20:41.520 --> 00:20:43.530
that has Italian language
that I can translate

00:20:43.530 --> 00:20:46.230
and think about Italian cuisine,
which is something I love.

00:20:46.230 --> 00:20:48.690
I also started listening
to Harry Potter in Italian

00:20:48.690 --> 00:20:50.900
which I didn't understand
a single word at first

00:20:50.900 --> 00:20:52.360
but then I got the Kindle version

00:20:52.360 --> 00:20:55.030
and I sort of followed along digitally

00:20:55.030 --> 00:20:57.620
and translated some words
while I was listening

00:20:57.620 --> 00:21:00.110
and now I can like
understand probably 80 to 90%

00:21:00.110 --> 00:21:03.470
of Harry Potter cause I love Harry Potter.

00:21:03.470 --> 00:21:06.110
Don't love JK Rowling
though, love Harry Potter.

00:21:06.110 --> 00:21:07.010
So make it fun.

00:21:07.010 --> 00:21:11.240
The last and final gate in
this more direct journey

00:21:11.240 --> 00:21:13.690
to be able to speak a language is the one

00:21:13.690 --> 00:21:15.310
that people who have
learned other languages

00:21:15.310 --> 00:21:16.920
have been waiting for me to say

00:21:16.920 --> 00:21:18.510
and they're angrily being like dude,

00:21:18.510 --> 00:21:20.900
you got to mention this and
I'm gonna say it, which is yes.

00:21:20.900 --> 00:21:22.660
Eventually, you have to learn the rules.

00:21:22.660 --> 00:21:23.600
You have to learn grammar.

00:21:23.600 --> 00:21:26.530
You have to learn pronunciation
and you have to refine it

00:21:26.530 --> 00:21:28.430
through drills and
through a lot of practice.

00:21:28.430 --> 00:21:30.640
Intentionally, I'm putting this last

00:21:30.640 --> 00:21:32.280
because I think you should too.

00:21:32.280 --> 00:21:34.830
The previous concepts
are way more important.

00:21:34.830 --> 00:21:39.100
Get your first 500 or
1,000 words memorized

00:21:39.100 --> 00:21:40.470
in your long-term memory.

00:21:40.470 --> 00:21:44.040
Start speaking every week
with a native speaker,

00:21:44.040 --> 00:21:48.220
make the journey fun, and then
start to think about grammar

00:21:48.220 --> 00:21:51.930
and start to think about all
of the rules and the irregulars

00:21:51.930 --> 00:21:54.210
and the syntax and the
direct object pronouns

00:21:54.210 --> 00:21:56.240
and the past participles.

00:21:56.240 --> 00:21:59.170
You can do that after
maybe two, three months

00:21:59.170 --> 00:22:00.610
of like being in the trenches.

00:22:00.610 --> 00:22:03.050
The best part about this
is that by that time,

00:22:03.050 --> 00:22:06.150
you'll be in a place where you
can communicate basic ideas

00:22:06.150 --> 00:22:08.260
so that when you do look at the grammar,

00:22:08.260 --> 00:22:10.600
it actually fits into the context

00:22:10.600 --> 00:22:13.140
that you've intuitively
developed of expression.

00:22:13.140 --> 00:22:14.600
It won't just be like in a vacuum

00:22:14.600 --> 00:22:16.150
of memorizing arbitrary rules,

00:22:16.150 --> 00:22:18.530
it'll actually apply to something.

00:22:18.530 --> 00:22:22.100
And that makes it way more
interesting to actually memorize.

00:22:22.100 --> 00:22:23.870
I didn't start really going into grammar

00:22:23.870 --> 00:22:25.140
until just recently,

00:22:25.140 --> 00:22:28.130
three months into the
process of learning Italian.

00:22:28.130 --> 00:22:30.130
And it makes a lot more sense.

00:22:30.130 --> 00:22:31.330
So that is what I've learned

00:22:31.330 --> 00:22:35.140
about learning another language,
it's like a video game.

00:22:35.140 --> 00:22:37.190
You don't need to gather
all the Korok seeds

00:22:37.190 --> 00:22:38.130
to beat the game.

00:22:38.130 --> 00:22:40.280
You don't, some people think you do.

00:22:40.280 --> 00:22:41.490
I'm not gonna listen to those people.

00:22:41.490 --> 00:22:44.080
For me, to beat the game,

00:22:44.080 --> 00:22:46.430
you just need to be able
to talk and be understood.

00:22:46.430 --> 00:22:49.180
This is my experience,
this is my version of it.

00:22:49.180 --> 00:22:50.013
And again, in the course,

00:22:50.013 --> 00:22:52.740
I go deeper into exactly
how this goes down.

00:22:52.740 --> 00:22:54.440
The course that me and Nathaniel did

00:22:54.440 --> 00:22:55.710
is like three and a half hours

00:22:55.710 --> 00:22:58.390
of like nitty gritty techniques
and all of this stuff,

00:22:58.390 --> 00:23:01.110
but the message is what
I've told you here,

00:23:01.110 --> 00:23:03.800
which is that there's a
better way to learn languages

00:23:03.800 --> 00:23:06.160
than the way we learned it in school.

00:23:06.160 --> 00:23:09.100
I will be studying Italian for years,

00:23:09.100 --> 00:23:12.120
but the beautiful thing is
next time I go to Italy,

00:23:12.120 --> 00:23:13.560
which who knows when that's gonna be,

00:23:13.560 --> 00:23:16.940
but at some point I'll go to
Italy, I'll show up to a cafe

00:23:16.940 --> 00:23:18.300
and I'll be able to speak.

00:23:18.300 --> 00:23:20.410
I'll be able to talk and be understood

00:23:21.360 --> 00:23:22.990
and that's what matters to me.

00:23:22.990 --> 00:23:26.250
So I hope that's what matters to you too.

00:23:26.250 --> 00:23:27.250
Thanks for watching.

00:23:28.194 --> 00:23:31.861
(speaking foreign language)

